The integral \( G(s) = \int_0^\infty e^{-st} \, dt \) is an improper integral. Evaluating it:

\[
G(s) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left[ \frac{e^{-st}}{-s} \right]_0^\infty = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left( \frac{1}{-s} e^{-st} - \frac{1}{-s} \right)
\]

For convergence, the exponential term must vanish as \( t \to \infty \), which happens when the real part of \( s \) is positive (\( \text{Re}(s) > 0 \)). Thus:

\[
G(s) = \frac{1}{s} \quad \text{for} \quad \text{Re}(s) > 0
\]

The Laplace transform of \( f(t) = 1 \) is:

\[
L\{1\} = \int_0^\infty 1 \cdot e^{-st} \, dt = \frac{1}{s} \quad \text{for} \quad \text{Re}(s) > 0
\]

The correct choice is:
**C. G(s) converges to (1 / s) for Re{s} > 0 and diverges otherwise. The required Laplace transform is L{1} = (1 / s), for Re{s} > 0.**
